INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(6366,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11672037','Canadian College of Medical Geneticists: clinical practice advisory document - responsibility to recontact for reinterpretation of clinical genetic testing.','Goh ES, Chad L, Richer J, Bombard Y, Mighton C, Agatep R, Lacaria M, Penny B, Thomas MA, Zawati MH, MacFarlane J, Laberge AM, Nelson TN.','2024','ccmg ccmg ccmg reinitiated uninformative reprioritise connotations reinterpreted ensuant reclassifications reclassifications downgrades reclassifications reclassifications reclassifications operationalisation unsolicited pulling reinterpret reinterpreted canvig canvig managements uninformative ccmg tnn tnn reclassifications reclassifications ccmg chooses reissue replaces ceasing irrevocable overridden operationalising pulling formalise pulling agreeable unsolicited reinterpreting reinterpret unsolicited prioritises prioritises fulsomely reissued obtains reinterpreted unsolicited reinterpreted reinterpreted reinterpreted unsolicited reinterpreted requisition flagging intitiated 198kb e2p2 ccmg linlea elaine suk elaine thp sickkids juricher cheo yvonne bombard yvonne bombard unityhealth chloe mighton chloe mighton unityhealth agatep ragatep sharedhealthmb lacaria mlacaria cheo blaine penny blaine lumiio maryann albertahealthservices zawati zawati jmacfarlane laberge laberge ssss gouv tanya tnelson cmpa acpm salinas maturo encephalopathies ejmg mecky plantinga s12920 plantinga birnie cmpa acpm burkard moscu gregor e1300 mgg3 campuzano sarquella falgueras ebiom quiat witkowski zouk e016195 vandyke esterling wijayatunge cherny chiodo glazer scn5a ranola jmo charnay blanck cerino dysf dysferlinopathy vallverdu prats alcalde sarquella jpm11030162 e83 e83 auckl s12282 gasperetti e003047 circgen reinterpreting s13045 iancu arteche s41525 mighton charames mighton mayers jmedgenet macklin atwal cerrato 0b013e318226fc15 kast wimberger slavin tongeren behrendt djy027 mcrae manrai funke nejmsa1507092 sorelle thodeson reinterpreting e182302 s13073 sarquella falgueras cesar channelopathies s00439 mersch pirzadeh wessinger molec e1275 mgg3 taber wmp hathaway margolin helm s12687 velthuizen luijt chek2 1100del hered s13053 beunders haver ejmg carrieri dheensa doheny dheensa carrieri venture ejmg axilbund codori s10689 peyron pelissier bejean mighton clausen s00439 mighton shickh uleryk multigene savatt azzariti ledbetter genomeconnect sirchia carrieri dheensa daoud ghani carrieri lucassen carrieri dheensa doheny vears niemiec scherr lindor malo spoonamore enserro e93 cphg dirksen ryr1 itga2b itgb3 bloodadvances fortuno distefano oza lott dulik feurstein bloodadvances mester pesaran krempely cdh1 mcknight karbassi angelman chora iacocca tichy ellard baple acgs acgs riggs gudmundsson berk carrieri loong canvig brenman thorogood zawati relearning bombard bombard mighton stranneheim lagerstedt magnusson s13073 s00246 longstaff flamenbaum e1500 shickh assamad bombard hayeems s41576 yauy lecoquierre baert desurmont mackley 198kb','Abstract Background Advances in technology and knowledge have facilitated both an increase in the number of patient variants reported and variants reclassified. While there is currently no duty to recontact for reclassified genetic variants, there may be a responsibility. The purpose of this clinical practice advisory document is to provide healthcare practitioners guidance for recontact of previously identified and classified variants, suggest methods for recontact, and principles to consider, taking account patient safety, feasibility, ethical considerations, health service capacity and resource constraints. The target audience are practitioners who order genetic testing, follow patients who have undergone genetic testing and those analysing and reporting genetic testing. Methods A multidisciplinary group of laboratory and ordering clinicians, patient representatives, ethics and legal researchers and a genetic counsellor from the Canadian Association of Genetic Counsellors reviewed the existing literature and guidelines on responsibility to recontact in a clinical context to make recommendations. Comments were collected from the Canadian College of Medical Geneticists (CCMG) Education, Ethics, and Public Policy, Clinical Practice and Laboratory Practice committees, and the membership at large. Results Following incorporation of feedback, and external review by the Canadian Association of Genetic Counsellors and patient groups, the document was approved by the CCMG Board of Directors. The CCMG is the Canadian organisation responsible for certifying laboratory and medical geneticists who provide medical genetics services, and for establishing professional and ethical standards for clinical genetics services in Canada. Conclusion The document describes the ethical and practical factors and suggests a shared responsibility between patients, ordering clinician and laboratory practitioners. Keywords: Delivery of Health Care, Genetic Testing WHAT IS ALREADY KNOWN ON THIS TOPIC There are inconsistencies and a lack of guidance on recontacting for reinterpretation of clinical genetic results. While there is no current duty to recontact, there may be responsibility to do so but ethical and practical considerations exist. WHAT THIS STUDY ADDS This document describes the ethical and practical factors that inform a suggested shared responsibility between patients, ordering clinicians and laboratory practitioners, providing guidance about scenarios, methods of recontact and potential next steps when there is limited health service capacity and resource constraint. HOW THIS STUDY MIGHT AFFECT RESEARCH, PRACTICE OR POLICY While this clinical practice advisory document was primarily written from a Canadian context, similar healthcare systems in other countries may use it to inform their practice or policies. The suggested future steps may serve as triggers for research and innovation. Introduction Advances in technology have facilitated an exponential increase in patient access to clinical genetic testing, as well as an increase in the number of variants reported for each patient. With this has come an increase in scientific understanding of gene-disease relationships and variant pathogenicity, which can change the interpretation of previously reported patients’ genetic results and subsequent medical management. There are inconsistencies and a lack of guidance, both within and outside of Canada, on recontacting patients and practitioners about changes in interpretation of previous genetic results; namely, who should initiate a request for reinterpretation of previous genetic testing results, how such requests should be reinitiated, and when. Definitions 1 , 6 used appear in table 1 . Table 1. Definitions. Duty The Canadian Medical Protective Association defines duty of care as ‘The doctor-patient relationship creates a series of obligations including the requirement, as appropriate, to attend, diagnose, advise, treat, and seek consultation’. 1 It is an obligation such as the duty of confidentiality that must be fulfilled and if not, one will be held liable. 1 There is a medicolegal implication involved in a duty. For the purposes of this document, we will focus on responsibility rather than a duty of the healthcare practitioner since the current evidence available is not strong enough to contribute to a clinical practice guideline. Former patient A patient with whom the healthcare practitioner has no current or ongoing relationship. Healthcare practitioner Healthcare practitioners include laboratory geneticists, clinical geneticists, genetic counsellors and other clinical specialists involved in the diagnosis and/or care of patients with genetic conditions. Where a distinction is needed, the wording used will be laboratory and ordering clinician. Reanalysis Reanalysis typically refers to large panel or genome-wide testing that initially yielded an uninformative result and there is a request to re-examine and reprioritise the data as a whole in light of newly emerging information. 2 3 Reclassification Reclassification occurs when a specific variant that has previously been classified based on applicable standard guidelines receives a new classification based on the evolution of scientific evidence. Recontact For the purposes of their systematic review, Otten et al 4 defined recontact as reaching out to a former patient with whom the clinical practitioner has no current or ongoing relationship. It can also be the laboratory reaching out to the ordering clinician in such case. Reinterpretation Reinterpretation refers to using new information such as a new clinical symptom, segregation data or new evidence to assess the previously collected data for a given patient. Updated biomedical literature and available databases can be used to aid with reinterpretation for the patient. 2 Reinterpretation of the data could lead to reclassification of a variant. Responsibility Healthcare practitioners have been tasked to protect and promote health as part of their professional responsibility. The notion of responsibility requires that one has both the knowledge and the ability to act on that knowledge. 5 Variant of uncertain significance (VUS) A genetic variant is classified as a VUS when the scientific evidence is insufficient to classify the variant as disease causing (likely pathogenic or pathogenic) or non-disease causing (likely benign or benign). 6 Open in a new tab Healthcare practitioners have a responsibility to remain up to date regarding available testing and technologies 7 and a duty to maintain confidentiality of the patient’s result. 7 They have been tasked to protect and promote health and well-being of the patient as part of their professional responsibility. The notion of responsibility requires that one has both the knowledge and the ability to act on that knowledge. 5 While there is currently no duty to recontact, there may be a responsibility. The key difference between duty and responsibility is that duty generally refers to an agreed set of legal obligations, while responsibility has ethical and/or moral connotations. This document focuses on responsibility since duty is related to legal obligation. Additionally, there are questions on whether a variant reinterpreted as a result of a request for one patient (whether a new testing request, or a request to revisit a previous interpretation) necessitates a ‘look back’ to identify all other patients who may have previously been identified to share the same variant. Furthermore, these considerations occur in the context of the Canadian medical genetics clinical community aiming to ensure all patients receive access to the benefits of genetic testing. The evolving nature of variant interpretation, current guidance, as well as patient and practitioner preferences, are all important considerations for ensuant guidance on recontacting patients for Canadian health professionals. Background Reclassification frequency The full literature review on the evolving nature of variant interpretation, and patient and practitioner perspectives can be found in online supplemental materials and is summarised below. 38 , 59 First, variant reclassification is not an uncommon occurrence. Reclassification occurs when a specific variant, or in some cases the gene(s) in which it is located, that has previously been classified one way receives a new classification based on the evolution of evidence. In 2015, the American College of Medical Genetics and Genomics (ACMG) and the Association for Molecular Pathology published guidelines for sequence variants that included a systematic classification with five tiers (pathogenic, likely pathogenic, variant of uncertain significance (VUS), likely benign and benign) 6 in an attempt to standardise classification; these have been further refined over time 60 and for specific conditions and genes. 61 , 70 Subsequently, guidelines have been published by other organisations including those by the UK Association for Clinical Genomic Science, 71 as well as the ACMG and Clinical Genome Resource for copy number variation. 72 Numerous studies have highlighted the evolving nature of variant interpretation as well as the time between the original reported classification and reclassification, and the impact on clinical care. 8 , 37 The overall rate of variant reclassification ranged from 3.6% (40 of 1103 variants) over a 4-year follow-up period 29 to 18.1% (268 of 1483 non-benign variants) over a 20-year follow-up period. 12 24 26 27 30 36 37 The high rate of reclassification over this timeframe is expected, given the dramatic increase in knowledge over the same time period as access to genetic testing and sequencing have increased, as well as the publication of previously mentioned guidelines. 6 71 72 Across studies, it was consistently observed that the majority of reclassifications, in particular VUS reclassifications, involved downgrades to a more benign classification, 12 24 26 27 30 36 37 which is not unexpected given the increased knowledge regarding the degree of benign variation on the population and due in part to the development of more robust control cohorts (eg, gnomAD). 73 Across studies that reported time between variant reclassifications, the average time between reclassifications ranged from 1 year to 1.86 years. 24 36 37 Impact of variant reclassifications on patients’ clinical care included changes to cascade testing, frequency and type of organ surveillance, and surgical recommendations. 24 27 28 37 38 Patient preferences Given the potential impacts on patient care, and in the best interest of the patient, it is important to consider patients’ preferences for being recontacted with updated genetic results. Patients’ interests are of central importance in this discussion. Research studies enrolling patients as research participants suggest there is largely a preference, and in some cases an expectation of recontact; however, this may be a biased sample. 3841 , 47 49 Eliciting preferences for recontact when results are initially returned is important in order to respect individuals’ preferences to not learn information and may decrease anxiety from learning updated results. 45 49 Participants’ views towards who bears the responsibility to initiate recontact varied, 40 , 4245 46 though across multiple studies, participants expressed support of a shared responsibility between the patient and practitioner. 40 , 4245 46 Practitioner perspectives Practitioners expressed a desirability for recontact 55 57 59 based on available studies, but time, resource and infrastructure constraints presented major barriers to its operationalisation. 55 There was a lack of clarity about who would provide the recontact and what the responsibilities were. 42 44 47 55 57 59 With the current approach, where the recontact could be triggered only when the ordering clinician was reviewing the case, 54 there was a fear of relying on clinician memory and missing patients. 55 Practitioners suggested that patients could in some cases share in the responsibility to recontact, which may promote autonomy and control over their health, while recognising that patients differ in their needs, education, capacity for autonomy and sociocultural backgrounds, which may mean that a shared responsibility is not appropriate in all cases. 55 Practitioners supported the need for a professional consensus on a duty or responsibility to recontact, and clarity around the resources and infrastructure required to carry out recontact. 55 Current context Current laboratory and clinic practice for reinterpretation and recontact appears to be extremely heterogeneous. 352 , 55 Various modalities and data storage systems have been employed to recontact patients. These include mailed letters, phone calls, patient portals, registries or electronic databases. 5152 57 , 59 There is existing variation in the practices of laboratories internationally. There are some laboratories that are ‘pushing’ information, meaning a laboratory issuing an unsolicited new report. Others are ‘pulling’ information based on an active request. A Canadian survey of genetic testing laboratories identified that laboratories did not actively reinterpret all variants, but would review variants if they discovered novel information in the literature or on request. 53 All responding laboratories reinterpreted variants if identified in a new proband and a sufficient period of time had elapsed since the previous interpretation, though the time period varied between laboratories, generally between 6 months and 12 months. 53 With regards to clinical practice, there is no Canadian specific data. Current international guidance Current guidance on reinterpretation and recontact in the clinical context have been published by the European Society of Human Genetics (ESHG), the Cancer Variant Interpretation Group UK (CanVIG-UK) and the ACMG. The ESHG suggests that while there is no duty to recontact, patients could be recontacted for updates that may have clinical or personal utility, if it is in the patient’s or family’s best interest, and there are sufficient resources to do so. 74 In the clinical context, the CanVIG-UK suggests a consensus framework for germline variants based on the nature and magnitude of the evidence shift and the classification score on which clinical and laboratory managements are recommended once it crosses an actionability threshold. 75 The ACMG recommends a shared responsibility for recontact between the patient, laboratory and clinician with the patient recontacting the practitioner to request updates. 76 Knoppers et al suggest reinterpretation, recontact, return of genetic variants and resource constraints are the four ‘R’s’ that are fundamental to this issue. 77 In a research context, the ASHG recommends that researchers should recontact participants to inform them of changes to or from pathogenic/likely pathogenic variants, as long as the patient has consented to result disclosure, can be identified, and the study is actively funded. 78 At the moment, there is no specific policy, law or guidance on recontact in the Canadian clinical setting. Need for Canadian guidance In the absence of Canadian specific law, policies or guidelines, and given the rapidly evolving landscape, there is benefit of providing some guidance for genetic healthcare practitioners in consideration of reinterpretation and recontact. For the purposes of this document, reinterpretation refers to using new information such as a new clinical symptom, segregation data or new evidence to reassess the previously reported variant(s). Updated biomedical literature and available databases can be used to aid with reinterpretation. 2 Reinterpretation of the data could lead to reclassification of a variant. Reanalysis however, typically refers to large panel or genome-wide testing that initially yielded an uninformative result and there is a request to re-examine the data as a whole in light of newly emerging information. 2 3 Reanalysis is out of scope for the purposes of this document and discussion. In this document, recontact is defined as reaching out to a former patient with whom the clinical practitioner has no current or ongoing relationship, as used in the systematic review by Otten et al , 4 or the laboratory practitioner reaching out to the clinic practitioner. If care is still being provided to the patient, updating them about a variant is an expected part of clinical practice and can be done at the next scheduled appointment or sooner based on clinical judgement. Purpose The purpose is to identify pertinent issues related to a clinical responsibility to recontact, in the context of the reclassification of DNA variants identified by genetic testing in patients of all ages with suspected monogenic disease within the Canadian healthcare system. In the absence of law, courts look at standard of practice to determine what should be discussed with patients when genetic testing is provided. Therefore, the intent of this document is to provide a clinical practice advisory document with points to consider, rather than recommendations and guidelines, as the current evidence base with regard to recontact is still uncertain and is expected to evolve over time. This document will provide guidance to healthcare practitioners on the responsibility to recontact patients about previously identified and classified variant(s), suggest methods for recontact and principles to consider taking account patient safety, feasibility, ethical considerations, health service capacity and resource constraints. The target audience for this document includes healthcare practitioners who order genetic testing or follow patients who have undergone genetic testing, and those analysing and reporting genetic testing in Canada. Methods The multidisciplinary Clinical Responsibility to Recontact for Reinterpretation of Clinical Genetic Testing working group included Canadian College of Medical Geneticists (CCMG) laboratory (ML, RA, TNN) and clinic (A-ML, EG, JR, LC) members, two patient representatives (BP and Alexander Galli), a genetic counsellor (JM), a legal representative (MZ), and a researcher with subject matter expertise (YB). The points were considered by subgroups within the working group from clinical (A-ML, EG, JM, JR, LC), ethics (CM, LC, MZ, YB) and laboratory expertise (MC, RA, TNN). Literature review (CM) as well as working group discussion and consensus were used to inform the document. The literature review aimed to identify articles reporting on the following topics: recommendations or guidelines for recontact, the frequency of variant reclassifications, impacts of variant reclassifications on patient care, and perspectives and experiences of patients and healthcare practitioners (clinical and laboratory) related to recontact. The literature review was conducted in 2021 through iterative searches of the PubMed database using search terms related to ‘genetic testing’, ‘recontact’ and ‘variant reclassification’ as well as specific topics of interest (eg, ‘patient perspectives’). The reference lists of relevant articles identified through the searches were reviewed to identify additional articles. A written summary of the evidence was presented to the working group. The document was reviewed by Clinical Practice, Education Ethics and Public Policy and Laboratory Practice committees and the broader CCMG community, with feedback incorporated. Two national patient support groups were consulted in the process. The Canadian Association of Genetic Counsellors also reviewed the document and provided endorsement. Important factors There are currently no Canadian specific laws, policies or guidelines for recontact. Given the rapidly evolving landscape, there is a need to provide some guidance for genetic healthcare practitioners in consideration of reinterpretation and recontact. Genetic counselling is integral to informing and supporting patients during this process, using shared decision-making. There are potential benefits to recontact and, thus, a desirability to work towards systems/processes that facilitate this. For instance, providing up-to-date reclassification information to a patient who has previously agreed to be recontacted by a practitioner with whom they still have a relationship would be ideal. However, situations become more complex when the healthcare practitioner and patient relationship has already ended, or that the laboratory chooses to reissue a report in the absence of a request from the ordering clinician. The working group agrees that recontact is ethically desirable, but practically difficult and resource-intensive given current healthcare delivery models. Thus, there is a need to balance the responsibility to recontact with ethical principles and logistical considerations. Ethical principles Beneficence and non-maleficence The primary ethical principle in support of recontact is beneficence. 74 76 78 Beneficence aims to maximise benefits. It is closely related to non-maleficence, which relates to capturing harms that can be avoided with recontact, as new information replaces old information and/or management. Non-maleficence also relates to the harms that may be caused by recontact, such as psychological distress. Considerations Providing a patient with an upgraded variant classification (ie, from VUS to likely pathogenic or pathogenic) could allow them to access necessary medical care such as high-risk screening or preventive interventions, access gene-specific treatment, inform reproductive planning, have a positive psychological impact from finding a diagnosis, decrease the anxiety related to a previously inconclusive result and inform risk management for family members. It may decrease the harms of unnecessary surveillance in family members who do not have the likely pathogenic or pathogenic variant when a diagnosis is known in the family. There may be additional benefits outside of the healthcare system such as enabling participation in research or clinical trials. A downgraded classification may also be of clinical benefit to patients (eg, in ceasing unnecessary screening or medical interventions or triggering additional testing to identify a new diagnosis). For some conditions, a downgraded classification could also benefit at-risk family members who would then qualify for ongoing surveillance in the absence of the familial variant. Even in the absence of clinical actions, recontact could confer personal benefit to patients by alleviating worry or concern. In contrast, some patients receiving downgraded classification may experience psychological distress due to increased uncertainty or when irrevocable decisions have been made based on previous classifications. In the case of a downgraded classification from pathogenic/likely pathogenic variant to a VUS, patients may have screening taken away from them that they had found reassuring. With any change in variant classification, there may be immediate management implications and sometimes there could be future management implications that may be of benefit to the patient and/or their relatives. Autonomy Respect for persons and respect for autonomy are important ethical considerations in support of systems that allow patients to choose whether they want to be recontacted. Even though the literature indicates most research participants value being recontacted, a subset would not want to be recontacted and their autonomy should be respected. 3840 , 42 45 It should be noted that there may be bias inherent in research that reflects participant opinion as those who choose to participate may differ from those who choose not to participate. This may not translate to preferences about being recontacted in the context of clinical care. Considerations The patient may or may not have consented for reinterpretation or recontact. Further, it is unclear whether patient non-consent ought to be overridden if there is reclassification that is deemed to be clinically significant. An issue for the practitioner (whether clinic or laboratory) is not knowing whether a patient has provided documented consent or would be willing to receive updates to their genetic results. Another issue is what to do for paediatric patients who may not have had capacity to consent initially but develop capacity in time. Equity Recontacting practices need to be equitable, sustainable and continually re-evaluated as the evidence base on outcomes and system impacts emerges. Operationalising equity in this space involves building equitable methods for recontact that do not solely rely on patients driving the request but may also involve healthcare practitioners initiating recontact. 79 Considerations Although all patients should have an opportunity to request a reinterpretation of their data, there may be factors that create inequity in recontact, such as lack of patient resources, awareness, and social determinants of health. For example, patients with higher health literacy or higher education may be more likely to contact their healthcare practitioner and request updates. Despite there being an ethical argument in support of recontact, there exist numerous practical barriers preventing systematic recontact of patients. Practical considerations Nature of the request The working group recognised that a request for reinterpretation may come from the patient, the clinician, as a result of testing indicated in another family member, or the testing laboratory may independently initiate reinterpretation based on internal protocols (see below for a description of ‘pushing’ and ‘pulling’ information). To facilitate reinterpretation, the patient should be aware and agree to the reinterpretation, the practitioner should agree it is reasonable to request reinterpretation based on the clinical scenario and/or laboratory/local policy. Clinical laboratories should develop a process to formalise and track requests. Results should be provided within a defined turnaround time. Lab policies should take into consideration the frequency of such requests and/or scenarios where reinterpretation is of reasonable utility. The patient who gets new testing should also be encouraged to share their classification with other family members so the family members can approach their ordering practitioner for explanation of their personal implications of the reclassification. Practically, the scenarios in which reinterpretation may be most warranted are If there is a pregnancy where interpretation is of clinical utility, in which case the patient could make the ordering practitioner aware of the pregnancy if they wish. To facilitate this, this option should be discussed with patients of reproductive age, or There may be a change in surgical management based on the reinterpretation, or A new family member comes forward for predictive testing, or Access to targeted therapies requires it, or If time has passed since the initial assessment. A suggested interval between the previous interpretation is when sufficient evidence exists that new information will be revealed through reinterpretation. This may be dependent on the genes or the technology used. The average reclassification period in the literature is typically at least 1–2 years, 24 36 37 3–5 years in a clinical context, 80 whereas one study found that the median time to VUS reclassification was 7 years. 81 Therefore, a review of a variant under 2 years may be less likely to be of clinical utility. While reinterpretation could also benefit from new clinical information, such as if the patient has new symptoms, consideration should be given to whether it is better to perform a reanalysis of the original data or consider offering new testing if indicated, which is outside the scope of this document. Clinician-initiated or patient-initiated requests (pulling information) prompted by the scenarios as listed above are encouraged and preferred. However, if information is laboratory-initiated (pushed) and would change clinical management, the ordering clinician should attempt to recontact the patient using reasonable efforts. For example, in most cases, contacting the patient two to three times at the most and if possible, using two methods (eg, phone and mail) or contacting the primary care practitioner may be reasonable. If the proband is deceased, contacting the next of kin (if known by the clinician) and disclosing the reclassification, if the next of kin is agreeable to receiving the information, would be suggested. Summary Healthcare practitioners should request (‘pull’) information from the clinical laboratory by requesting a reinterpretation if time has passed since the initial assessment or there is a clinical indication for which there may be clinical utility for reinterpretation such as a pregnancy. If information is provided unsolicited (‘pushed’), from the clinical laboratory to the healthcare practitioner, the healthcare practitioner should attempt to recontact the patient using reasonable efforts if the change in variant classification would have clinical utility. Practice variation and evolving evidence Despite attempts at standardisation, clinical judgement is integral to variant classification. Furthermore, differences exist between clinical standards and guidelines used, as well as the information available to the laboratory at the time of classification. Together, these may result in discrepancy in how different laboratories classify a variant (eg, one laboratory may classify a variant as VUS and another as pathogenic/likely pathogenic). 25 This may lead to differences in management, clinical recommendations or clinical testing for family members. Summary A variant’s classification is only part of its clinical interpretation, and the practitioner should consider all relevant laboratory and clinical data to make a judgement on the overall relevance to the patient. Resource allocation Ensuring the resources needed for recontact are proportionate and sustainable 74 is important within a publicly funded healthcare system with limited resources. It is important to realise that efforts placed in reinterpreting an individual’s variant may be efforts taken away from another patient who has not had the benefit of initial testing. Laboratories and clinics need to consider whether the best use of resources is to reinterpret variants from patients who have already been tested, recontact patients for whom they received an unsolicited variant reinterpretation, or to put resources towards testing and diagnosing new patients. If one prioritises reinterpretation, the same patients get repeated access to genetics service resources. If one prioritises new tests, more patients get access to genetics service resources. As more individuals are tested, more variants will be identified and subsequently require reinterpretation and thus the balance may shift. Moreover, consideration must be given to whether the healthcare system has the capacity to manage reinterpretation requests for only a given patient, or also for others with the same variant. For example, if a variant is reclassified, it remains unclear whether there is a responsibility by the laboratory or clinic to assess whether other individuals within their practice area have also had the same variant reported. Summary Resource use in the context of a publicly funded healthcare system needs to be proportionate and sustainable. Infrastructure and process Resource use requires the proper infrastructure and processes. Issues include the ability and costs of searching for other patients with the same variant, especially given that records may not be electronically searchable, and the responsibility of the laboratory or clinic once a reclassification is known. In general, it is ideal that recontact is driven by the patient or ordering clinician through the initiation of a new referral/contact to assess the clinical question where new actionable information could be helpful. Before requesting reassessment of variant classification and interpretation, the ordering clinician is responsible for ensuring that: it is clinically relevant to request reassessment; that the request is in keeping with laboratory policy; that the patient has agreed to the reassessment; and that updated clinical information has been provided to the laboratory, as relevant. Laboratories should establish policy and processes for reassessment of variant classification and interpretation requests, including turnaround time, and make these available to ordering practitioners. When a variant is reclassified in the context of either a new patient, new guidelines, or revisiting a variant classification at patient/practitioner request, laboratories and clinics do not have a responsibility to look for previous patients and provide updated variant classification, due to the lack of infrastructure to fulsomely achieve this goal at this time. There is also no clear responsibility to recontact other family members who are also patients of the ordering clinician, though this might be desirable in many circumstances. Instead, families should be encouraged to share their updated classifications with each other. To facilitate this, a written letter that clearly documents the reinterpretation, impact on other family members and any recommended course of action, if applicable, that the family member could take, should be shared with the patient. Recognising that there may be varying policy and practice among laboratories, ordering clinicians are encouraged to review the policy and practice of testing laboratories and discuss these with their patient as part of the test ordering and results session. Summary The practitioner is responsible for ensuring that: it is clinically relevant to request reassessment; that the request is in keeping with laboratory policy; that the patient has agreed to the reassessment; and that updated clinical information has been provided to the laboratory, as relevant. There is no responsibility for laboratories or clinics to review existing historical charts or databases and provide updated variant classification to all patients for which that given variant has been previously reported. Families are encouraged to share updated classifications with each other, but new reports should not be reissued automatically for family members. Laboratories should establish policy and processes for reassessment of variant classification and interpretation requests, including turnaround time, and make these available to ordering practitioners. Ordering healthcare practitioners are encouraged to review the policy and practice of testing laboratories and discuss these with their patient as part of the test ordering. Consent and discharge Practical considerations related to a recontact consent form is that it may be stored in different locations, which may or may not be available to relevant healthcare practitioners. While it may be previously documented that the patient wanted recontact, practically there may be variation in where the documentation exists, such as in the clinical note not available to the laboratory, or a laboratory consent form not available to the clinic. If there is an electronic medical record, ideally it would exist there. Furthermore, the patient’s consent may change over time. Patients should be made aware of the possibility for reinterpretation at the time the healthcare practitioner obtains the initial consent for testing and orders the test. Institutions may have their own preferences for consent forms that may be context-specific. Core elements of a pan-Canadian consent form and an example of potential language to use have been suggested. 82 If a reinterpreted report is provided and recontact is of potential clinical utility, especially if the change has important implications for medical management, then an effort should be made to ask the patient if they would like to learn updated information. From the patient’s perspective, they may wish to only learn specific types of information (eg, only clinically actionable information). Furthermore, consideration should be given to the necessary infrastructure to support the management of such information, including up-to-date patient contact information, next of kin (should the patient be deceased at the time of recontact) and updated consent when capable if they were not capable at the time of initial testing. It is acknowledged that in most cases, patients who have consented to return of results at the time of the original consent would consent to recontact for the same type of results. An unclear situation can arise when the laboratory issues an unsolicited reinterpreted report, and the ordering clinician no longer has a responsibility for the patient because they have been discharged. The ordering clinician who is no longer responsible for the patient should either try to contact the patient directly or contact a clinician of record who is currently responsible for their care. Furthermore, since laboratories only send reports to the ordering physician, genetics practitioners may be called on by their colleagues to interpret the clinical implications of that reinterpreted report. Summary Patients should be made aware of the possibility for reinterpretation at the time of initial consent and ideally patient consent for recontact should be documented in the clinical note and/or the electronic medical record. If a reinterpreted report is provided and recontact is required, then an effort should be made to ask the patient if they would like to learn updated information. If an unsolicited, reinterpreted report is obtained, reasonable effort should be made to alert the patient and the practitioner who is currently responsible for their care, especially if the change has important implications for medical management. Conclusion and future steps Shared responsibility The recontact of patients with information that may have clinical utility is desirable in many circumstances. In light of the resource constraints in the Canadian public healthcare system, the suggestion is that this is not a duty or obligation on the part of healthcare practitioners, but a shared responsibility between patients, ordering clinician and laboratories until more robust consensus, infrastructure and systems exist. 45 55 79 For the purposes of this document, we focused on responsibility rather than a duty since the current evidence available is not strong enough to contribute to a clinical practice guideline. Under a model of shared responsibility, the following responsibilities may enable patient-centred care: Patient responsibility Patients should maintain up-to-date contact information and preferences related to recontact with the ordering practitioner. If a new situation may justify a re-evaluation of the variant (eg, a new pregnancy, etc), they should contact their practitioner to initiate this, ideally via re-referral from a family physician. Parents are responsible for informing their children regarding these responsibilities once they are capable to provide their own consent. Also, if the patient is recontacted with a reclassification, they would be encouraged to share their findings with other family members to find out whether there are any implications for their family member’s management. Ordering clinician responsibility Ordering clinicians should discuss with patients the possibility a test’s interpretation might change in time, the current process for recontact, the current limitations of recontact pathways including that there is no practitioner duty to search for other affected patients in the event a variant is reclassified, that recontact can only occur if up-to-date contact information is available, and how and when a patient may initiate recontact via a re-referral if a reinterpretation is desired. Non-genetics care practitioners should be aware of the dynamic nature of result interpretation and similarly be aware that a reinterpretation could be requested in time. When requesting reassessment of variant classification and interpretation, practitioners are responsible for ensuring that the request meets laboratory policy, and that the patient has agreed to the request being made. Laboratory responsibility Laboratories should establish a policy and associated processes for requesting reassessment of variant classification and interpretation, including under which circumstances requests can be made, how to make requests and expected turnaround time; these should be made available to ordering practitioners. There is, however, no current duty to search for updated information about a variant systematically, or for other patients who have the same variant when a variant is reclassified. The use of a reinterpretation requisition allows laboratories to process and track these volumes over time. Potential future steps A shared responsibility will require working towards practical tools that can manage this flow of information. Patient portals, 49 51 secure messaging and electronic medical records may be helpful tools for recontact, if patient access and availability are not an issue, and there is existing infrastructure. Managing patients’ consent preferences for future recontact may be ideally situated within electronic medical records, alongside digital tools to enable patient empowerment and informed decisions to support consent. 83 84 Ongoing patient access to their medical records could help facilitate situations where the patient has moved out of the province or the country. Laboratory infrastructure and resources for reclassification are necessary, which may include: a centralised, comprehensive and sustainable variant database with connections to the patient chart and the laboratory information system; automated mechanism(s) 85 to identify variants requiring reclassification based on established frequency and/or updated variant information and/or updated patient-specific clinical indications; automated mechanisms to generate the report within laboratory information systems once reclassification has occurred; as well as the associated ability for automated flagging of newly published articles on relevant genes/variants for review that may trigger a variant reclassification. National data sharing between laboratories will help aid in obtaining consensus for variant classifications and ensuring that all laboratories have access to the same information. In addition, potential enablers to help with a sustainable recontact process includes funding for clinic and laboratory human health resources, more family doctors to help access genetic services, and education directed towards patients and primary care practitioners about the benefits of reinterpretation and reimbursement for the work related to the process of reinterpretation. This may allow for a more equitable distribution of the shared model of responsibility that is acknowledged to be heavily patient-intitiated. 86 The summary appears in box 1 . Further research and evidence are required before there can be a definitive clinical practice guideline. This clinical practice advisory document will be reviewed and updated through the standard document review process by the Ethics, Education and Public Policy committee. Box 1. Summary. Patient responsibilities: Patients should maintain up-to-date contact information and preferences related to recontact with the ordering practitioner in case there is laboratory-initiated or pushing of information. Patients should contact their practitioner if a new situation may justify a re-evaluation or ask for a new referral if they are no longer an active patient. Parents are responsible for informing their children regarding these responsibilities once they are capable to provide their own consent. Ordering clinician responsibility: Ordering clinicians should discuss with patients: A test’s interpretation might change in time. The current process for recontact. The current limitations of recontact pathways. Situations that could prompt recontact from the patient (eg, pregnancy, etc) Ways a patient may initiate recontact if a reinterpretation is desired. Non-genetics care practitioners should be aware that reinterpretation is dynamic and could be requested in time with a re-referral to genetics. Practitioners are responsible for ensuring that any request for reinterpretation has been agreed to by the patient of record and meets laboratory policy. Laboratory responsibility: Laboratories should establish policy and associated processes about requests for variant reinterpretation, and make these available to ordering practitioners, There is no current duty to search for updated information about a variant systematically, or for other patients who have 